    <h1>Membership Interest Transfer and Joinder Agreement</h1>
    <div class="cc-subtitle">Cornelius Codes LLC &bull; Illinois Secretary of State File No. 13382174</div>

    <p>
      <strong>THIS MEMBERSHIP INTEREST TRANSFER AND JOINDER AGREEMENT</strong> (this “Agreement”) is entered into as of
      <input type="text" id="ccEffDate" class="cc-input" value="November 18, 2026" />,
      by and among <strong>Ellen Amanda Allt</strong> (“Transferor”), <strong>Cornelius Codes LLC</strong>, an Illinois limited liability company (the “Company”), and the undersigned parties listed on <strong>Exhibit A</strong> (each, a “Transferee” and collectively, the “Transferees”).
    </p>

    <h2>1. Background and Entity Information</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Company Details:</strong> Cornelius Codes LLC is a limited liability company organized and existing under the Illinois Limited Liability Company Act, 805 ILCS 180/1-1 <em>et seq.</em> (the “Illinois Act”), registered with the Illinois Secretary of State under File Number <strong>13382174</strong>, with its principal office located at <strong>4601 N Paulina Street, Ste 402, Chicago, IL 60640</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Current Ownership:</strong> Transferor currently holds a forty-nine percent (49%) membership interest in the Company (the “Transferor Interest”).</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Product, Open-Source Upstream &amp; Project Origin:</strong> The Company’s primary commercial product is <strong>Google Laws</strong>, which is a proprietary derivative work built upon and derived from the open-source <strong>GovBot</strong> codebase (<code>github.com/chihacknight/govbot</code> and related legislative data repositories). The Parties acknowledge that:
        <ol>
          <li>The GovBot project originated out of civic technology sessions hosted by <strong>Chi Hack Night Inc.</strong>, an Illinois not-for-profit corporation, and pursuant to the bylaws and policies of Chi Hack Night Inc., Chi Hack Night Inc. claims and holds no intellectual property rights, ownership, or proprietary interest over projects or code created during its seminars or events; and</li>
          <li>While upstream open-source repositories of GovBot remain publicly accessible and include contributions from non-member community collaborators (including, without limitation, Divyam, Ramisa Rahima, Michael Neuman, and Dan Grumieaux), <strong>Google Laws</strong> and all proprietary extensions, commercial deployments, branding, and derivative works thereof are the sole and exclusive property of Cornelius Codes LLC.</li>
        </ol>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Documented Sweat Equity &amp; Stewardship Consideration:</strong> Each individual Transferee has performed substantial development, data engineering, observability, or civic-tech operational work on the <strong>GovBot</strong> ecosystem (collectively, the “Services”), including: backend architecture, Model Context Protocol (MCP) server development, and A–F state data quality frameworks (<em>Sarta J</em>); real-time Fable 5 dashboards, Witness Slips/Hearings RSS trackers, social media alert bots, and <em>Know Your Ballot</em> deployments (<em>Frankie S.</em>); scraper failure classification, OpenStates session auto-pausing, self-hosted runner infrastructure, and state bill scraper maintenance (<em>Tamera</em>); <code>jsonl-action-logs</code> consolidation and backend data pipelines (<em>Taha Husain</em>); GitHub Actions observability, metrics/logging, and Grafana alerting infrastructure (<em>Nate Gross</em>); and Witness Slips architecture, ATProtocol Spaces data segmentation, distributed scraping designs, and mascot visual identity direction (<em>Edwin Chalas Cuevas</em>). In addition, the Parties wish to dedicate one equal share as a <strong>Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share</strong> (grounded in the biblical principle of tithing and firstfruits offerings, <em>Proverbs 3:9</em> and <em>Malachi 3:10</em>) to be held and appropriated for qualified charitable organizations in Illinois.</li>
      <li><strong>Purpose:</strong> In compensation for the Services rendered, to honor the charitable dedication, and in exchange for the repayment obligations and mutual covenants set forth herein, Transferor desires to transfer and assign the entire forty-nine percent (49%) Transferor Interest in seven (7) equal shares (7.00% each) as set forth in <strong>Exhibit A</strong>, and the Company desires to admit the Transferees as Non-Voting Economic Members pursuant to the Illinois Act.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>2. Transfer and Nature of Membership Interests</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Equal Seven-Way Allocation (49% Total):</strong> Subject to the terms of this Agreement, Transferor hereby transfers, assigns, and conveys her entire forty-nine percent (49%) membership interest divided into <strong>seven (7) equal shares of seven percent (7.00%) each</strong> (<code>49.00% &divide; 7 = 7.00%</code> per share) to the six (6) individual contributors and the designated <strong>Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share</strong> as set forth in <strong>Exhibit A</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Retained Interest:</strong> Immediately following this transfer, Transferor shall retain a <strong>zero percent (0.00%)</strong> membership interest in the Company.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Administration and Appropriation of the Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share:</strong>
        <ul>
          <li>The 7.00% <strong>Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share</strong> shall be held in trust or administered by the Company for charitable distribution and shall be appropriated as necessary by a <strong>majority vote of the voting membership ownership</strong> of Cornelius Codes LLC.</li>
          <li>Any recipient organization selected to receive funds or distributions from the Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share must be a <strong>registered domestic Illinois corporation</strong> duly registered and in good standing as a charitable organization with the <strong>Office of the Illinois Attorney General</strong> (pursuant to the Illinois Charitable Trust Act, 760 ILCS 55/, and/or the Solicitation for Charity Act, 225 ILCS 460/). Federal tax-exempt recognition under Section 501(c)(3) of the Internal Revenue Code is <strong>not</strong> a requirement for eligibility.</li>
        </ul>
      </li>
      <li><strong>Full Economic Rights (Profits, Losses, Capital, and Assets):</strong> Pursuant to 805 ILCS 180/15-1 and 805 ILCS 180/30-1, each transferred membership interest carries full economic rights corresponding to its respective 7.00% percentage interest, including the right to receive allocations of the Company’s profits and losses, distributions of cash or property, and distributions of capital and assets upon liquidation or dissolution of the Company.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-Voting Status (No Management or Voting Power):</strong> Pursuant to 805 ILCS 180/15-1(c), all membership interests transferred under this Agreement (including the Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share) are <strong>strictly non-voting</strong>. Transferees shall have no voting rights, no right to participate in the management or conduct of the Company’s business, and no authority to bind the Company or inspect records beyond the minimum mandatory disclosures required under 805 ILCS 180/10-15 that cannot be waived by an operating agreement. All management and voting authority remains vested exclusively in the voting membership ownership and Managing Member(s) as set forth in the Company's Operating Agreement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>3. Succession of Majority Voting Power and Probate Continuity</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Succession Upon Death, Incapacity, or Probate:</strong> In the event of the death, legal incapacity, or transfer through probate, estate administration, or trust administration of the holder of the fifty-one percent (51%) voting membership interest in Cornelius Codes LLC, the full <strong>fifty-one percent (51%) voting power</strong> and associated management authority in the Company shall automatically be assigned to and vested in <strong>Erica Michele Cornelius</strong>, subject to and in coordination with the administrative and execution authority of <strong>Andrew McCue, CPA</strong>, acting in his capacity as Executor under the Last Will and Testament of the majority membership interest holder.</li>
      <li><strong>Binding Effect on Operating Agreement:</strong> All parties to this Agreement expressly consent to this succession arrangement and agree that it shall be incorporated into and binding upon the Company’s Operating Agreement pursuant to 805 ILCS 180/15-1 and 805 ILCS 180/30-20 (Power of Estate of Deceased or Incompetent Member).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>4. Consideration, Intellectual Property Assignment &amp; California Labor Law Carve-Out</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Transferee Services &amp; Charitable Dedication:</strong> The sole and sufficient consideration provided by the six individual Transferees is the Services already performed on the <strong>GovBot</strong> project, and the consideration for the Firstfruits &amp; Tithe Charitable Share is the irrevocable dedication of such share to eligible Illinois charitable organizations. No cash payment or initial capital contribution is required from any Transferee.</li>
      <li><strong>Present Assignment &amp; Commercial License of Intellectual Property:</strong> To the extent any Transferee created, authored, or contributed code, data models, scrapers, dashboards, observability pipelines, visual designs, documentation, or other work product to <strong>GovBot</strong> or <strong>Google Laws</strong>, each Transferee hereby: (a) irrevocably assigns, transfers, and conveys to Cornelius Codes LLC all right, title, and interest worldwide in and to all derivative commercial rights in <strong>Google Laws</strong> (including all copyrights, patent rights, trade secrets, and moral rights to the fullest extent permitted by law); and (b) to the extent any underlying contribution remains subject to an upstream open-source license, grants the Company a perpetual, irrevocable, worldwide, royalty-free, sublicensable license to commercialize, modify, and incorporate such contribution into <strong>Google Laws</strong> without restriction.</li>
      <li><strong>California Labor Code &sect; 2870 &amp; Illinois Employee Patent Act Notice:</strong> To the extent California law is ever deemed applicable to any Transferee residing or working in California, this intellectual property assignment <strong>does not apply</strong> to any invention or work product that qualifies fully for exclusion under <strong>California Labor Code Section 2870</strong> (or <strong>765 ILCS 1060/2</strong> under Illinois law)—specifically, any invention developed entirely on the Transferee’s own time without using the Company’s equipment, supplies, facilities, or trade secret information, except for those inventions that either (a) relate at the time of conception or reduction to practice to the Company’s business (including GovBot and Google Laws) or actual or demonstrably anticipated research or development, or (b) result from any work performed by the Transferee for the Company.</li>
      <li><strong>Tax Acknowledgment &amp; Independent Status:</strong> Each individual Transferee acknowledges that: (a) they contributed to GovBot as an independent civic-tech collaborator and not as a W-2 employee or contractor of the Company; and (b) receiving a membership interest in exchange for services may result in taxable income under federal, Illinois, and/or California tax laws, and each Transferee agrees to be solely responsible for their own personal tax reporting and obligations (including any election under IRC Section 83(b), if applicable).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>5. Joinder to Operating Agreement &amp; Securities Law Compliance</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Admission as Non-Voting Members:</strong> Upon execution of this Agreement, satisfaction of the Illinois notarization condition precedent in Article 14, and consent of the Managing Member, each Transferee is admitted as a Non-Voting Member of the Company and agrees to be bound by all terms, conditions, and transfer restrictions of the Company’s Operating Agreement, as amended from time to time (including any right of first refusal held by the Company prior to any third-party transfer of economic interests).</li>
      <li><strong>No Additional Capital Calls:</strong> In accordance with 805 ILCS 180/20-5, unless otherwise agreed in writing, Transferees shall have no obligation to make additional capital contributions to the Company.</li>
      <li><strong>Federal, Illinois &amp; California Securities Exemptions:</strong> Each Transferee represents and warrants that: (a) they are acquiring their non-voting membership interest for their own account for investment and not with a view to public resale or distribution; (b) they have a pre-existing substantive personal or working relationship with the Company and its Managing Member through the GovBot project; and (c) the issuance of these interests is exempt from registration under Section 4(a)(2) of the Securities Act of 1933, Section 4.G / 4.H of the Illinois Securities Law of 1953 (815 ILCS 5/4), and California Corporations Code Section 25102(f) (limited offering exemption).</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>6. Representations and Warranties</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Transferor:</strong> Transferor represents that they are the sole legal and beneficial owner of the Transferor Interest and have full authority to transfer these interests free and clear of any liens or encumbrances.</li>
      <li><strong>Individual Transferees:</strong> Each individual Transferee represents and warrants that: (a) they performed the Services on GovBot free of any conflicting third-party or employer IP claims; (b) they have had an opportunity to review the Company’s organizational records and ask questions of the Managing Member; and (c) they have been advised of their right to consult independent legal and tax counsel prior to executing this Agreement.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>7. General Provisions</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Governing Law &amp; Internal Affairs Doctrine:</strong> Under the internal affairs doctrine recognized by both Illinois and California courts (see <em>805 ILCS 180/1-1 et seq.</em> and <em>Cal. Corp. Code &sect; 17708.01</em>), the organization, internal affairs, membership rights, voting power, and fiduciary duties of Cornelius Codes LLC shall be governed exclusively by the laws of the <strong>State of Illinois</strong>, without regard to conflict-of-law principles.</li>
      <li><strong>Forum Selection &amp; Consent to Jurisdiction:</strong> Any action, suit, or proceeding arising out of or relating to this Agreement or the internal affairs of the Company shall be brought exclusively in the state or federal courts located in <strong>Cook County, Illinois</strong>. Each party irrevocably submits to the personal jurisdiction of such courts and waives any objection based on <em>forum non conveniens</em> or venue, except to the extent mandatory non-waivable statutory protections under California law are held by a court of competent jurisdiction to apply to a California resident.</li>
      <li><strong>Severability &amp; Blue-Pencil Reformation:</strong> If any provision of this Agreement is held invalid or unenforceable under Illinois or California law, such provision shall be reformed to the minimum extent necessary to render it valid and enforceable, and the remaining provisions shall continue in full force and effect.</li>
      <li><strong>Entire Agreement &amp; Counterparts:</strong> This Agreement (together with the Company’s Operating Agreement) constitutes the entire agreement among the parties with respect to the subject matter hereof. Subject to the mandatory Illinois notarization condition precedent in Article 14, this Agreement may be executed in counterparts, including via electronic signature in compliance with the Illinois Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (815 ILCS 333/) and the California Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (Cal. Civ. Code &sect; 1633.1 <em>et seq.</em>), each of which shall be deemed an original.</li>
    </ul>

