    <h2>11. Transfer Restrictions, Right of First Refusal &amp; Community Property Protections</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Prohibition on Unauthorized Transfers:</strong> No Transferee may sell, assign, pledge, encumber, hypothecate, or otherwise transfer all or any portion of their non-voting economic interest (whether voluntarily, involuntarily, or by operation of law) without the prior written consent of the Managing Member, except in strict compliance with the Right of First Refusal set forth in this Article 11. Any attempted transfer in violation of this Article 11 shall be null and void <em>ab initio</em>.</li>
      <li><strong>Company Right of First Refusal (ROFR):</strong> Prior to any proposed bona fide sale or transfer of a Transferee’s economic interest to a third party, the transferring Transferee shall deliver written notice to the Company specifying the proposed transferee, percentage interest, purchase price, and material terms. The Company shall have an exclusive option for thirty (30) days following receipt of such notice to purchase all or any portion of the offered interest on the same economic terms (or, at the Company’s election, at the fair market value determined by the Company’s independent CPA).</li>
      <li><strong>Charging Order Sole Remedy &amp; Involuntary Transfers:</strong> In accordance with 805 ILCS 180/30-20, the exclusive remedy of any judgment creditor, bankruptcy trustee, or divorcing spouse of a Transferee shall be a charging order against the Transferee’s distributional interest. No creditor, assignee, or former spouse shall acquire any right to inspect Company records, participate in management, or force a dissolution or liquidation of the Company.</li>
